# A foreign marker named by its qualified spelling in an arm's pattern, in a
# file whose only other use of the import is a call.
#
# Should: compile and print "false". The arm `fn null? json/json_null` does
# claim json_null (add `{null? json/json_null}` to the print and the program
# runs and prints "false true"), so the import is in use.
#
# Does: `kanso play qualified_marker_in_pattern.kso` refuses with
#   error[unused]: unused import "std/json"
# A qualified name in a pattern does not count as a use of the import. Delete
# the import and the message turns into `unused binding json/json_null`: the
# same pattern is now read as a fresh binding with a slash in its name. One
# spelling means two different things depending on an import line, and
# neither diagnostic says which reading the compiler took. The bare spelling
# `fn null? json_null` gets the same unused-import refusal. What works is
# ascribing the wildcard: `fn null? _:json/json_null`.
import "std/json"

fn null? json/json_null
  true

fn null? _
  false

print "{null? 5}"
